{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document: Role-Based Access Control (Admin vs. Regular User)\n\n## Problem\nThe Ledger API currently does not differentiate between administrative users and regular users. As a small Flask service tracking user accounts and payments, all authenticated users have identical access levels. Without permission boundaries, administrative actions cannot be secured against unauthorized access by regular users.\n\n## Goals\n- Introduce a two-tier role system: `admin` and `regular` (`user`).\n- Store the role attribute directly on the user entity.\n- Protect administrative operations by restricting endpoint access to users with the `admin` role.\n- Ensure regular users can continue normal ledger and account workflows without interruption.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- A complex, multi-tiered, or dynamic permission system (e.g., custom role definitions, fine-grained object-level access control lists).\n- Self-service admin role requests or multi-tenant permission structures.\n\n## User Impact\n- **Regular Users**: Retain access to their standard account and payment views, but will receive authorization errors (e.g., 403 Forbidden) if attempting to perform administrative actions.\n- **Admin Users**: Gain access to administrative endpoints and functions necessary to manage the Ledger API service.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- Which specific endpoints and ledger operations in `app.py` should be restricted to admin-only access versus kept accessible to regular users?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document: Role-Based Access Control\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Data Model (`models.py`)**: Add a `role` column (string or enum) to the `User` model with a default value (e.g., `'user'`). Setting a default prevents existing records from having `NULL` values and failing authorization checks.\n- **Authorization Layer (`app.py`)**: Implement an authorization decorator or helper (e.g., `@admin_required`) to check the current user's role before executing restricted route handlers.\n- **Service Context**: The application is built on `flask==3.0.0` and `flask-sqlalchemy==3.1.1` as defined in `requirements.txt`.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Schema Migration (`models.py`, `requirements.txt`)**: No database migration framework (such as Flask-Migrate or Alembic) is present in `requirements.txt`. Adding a column to `User` in `models.py` risks schema divergence or data loss in existing databases unless a migration strategy is established.\n- **Admin Bootstrapping (`app.py`)**: Because all users default to standard roles, a bootstrapping mechanism (e.g., a Flask CLI command, environment-variable seed, or migration script) must exist to designate at least one initial admin user.\n- **Endpoint Scope (`app.py`)**: Ambiguity regarding which routes require admin privileges must be resolved to avoid either locking regular users out of account tracking or leaving sensitive endpoints exposed.\n- **Session Invalidation**: When a user's role is modified or revoked, active sessions or tokens must reflect the change promptly to prevent unauthorized actions during the remainder of the session.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- How should the initial admin user be created or bootstrapped (e.g., Flask CLI command in `app.py`, startup environment variable, or manual database seed)?\n- Should a database migration tool such as Flask-Migrate or Alembic be introduced into `requirements.txt`, or will schema changes be handled manually?\n- How should active user sessions or authentication tokens be refreshed or invalidated when a user's role changes?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Estimated Effort**: 2-4 days (up to 8 days worst case depending on endpoint audit and migration tooling requirements)."}